@startuml
title What each venue asks you to trust
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam PackageBackgroundColor #F7FAFF
skinparam PackageBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam ComponentBackgroundColor #EEF5FF
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam ComponentFontColor #1F3A5F
skinparam ArrowColor #4A78B5
skinparam ArrowFontColor #1F3A5F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #F7FAFF
skinparam NoteBorderColor #4A78B5

package "Trusted party" {
  [CEX operator\nruns the engine, holds the funds,\nsells the colocation] as A
  [Hyperliquid validators\n> 2/3 of stake honest;\nordering rule is in execution] as B
  [dYdX v4 validators\n> 2/3 of stake honest;\nresting book is not verifiable\nfrom chain state alone] as C
  [GMX oracle + keepers\nsigned prices delivered on time;\nLPs bear the traders' PnL] as D
}

package "Failure that follows" {
  [Order reordering or a\nfront-run you cannot detect] as FA
  [A colluding stake majority sets\nthe oracle that liquidates you] as FB
  [A proposer's view of the book\ncannot be replayed from state] as FC
  [Stale or gapped oracle prices;\nkeepers that do not execute] as FD
}

A --> FA
B --> FB
C --> FC
D --> FD

note bottom of FA
  Every design moves the trust somewhere.
  None removes it. The useful question is
  which failure you can observe after the fact.
end note
@enduml
